WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.300
A former NFL player becomes the quintessential
tall tough guy.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.860
A star trades his badge for border terriers.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.520
And one cadet transitions into a lifelong
Santa Claus.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.740
The stars of "Police Academy" moved on to
other things, but which of them were bigger

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:15.740
or better?

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.129
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:17.498 --> 00:00:22.100
Steve Guttenberg was already gaining plenty
of traction in his screen acting career, thanks

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.480
to films like "Diner" and TV series like "Billy"
by the time the role of Carey Mahoney in "Police

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.880
Academy" came to him.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.720
Guttenberg's lovable scoundrel performance
in the film helped make it a hit — and helped

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.310
launch him into some of the biggest films
of the 1980s.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.410
Throughout the rest of the decade, he starred
in three "Police Academy" sequels as well

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.370
as "Short Circuit," "Cocoon," and "3 Men and
a Baby."

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:48.660
He continued working regularly in the 1990s
in comedy and family films like "The Big Green,"

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.570
"Home for the Holidays," and "It Takes Two,"
and in the 2000s he won a role in cult favorite

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.350
TV series "Veronica Mars."

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.530
Today he continues acting regularly, and in
2017 he started appearing alongside Dwayne

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.940
Johnson in the HBO series "Ballers."

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.710
In 2002, he moved behind the camera, making
his directorial debut with an adaptation of

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.880
James Kirkwood Jr.'s play "P.S.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.040
Your Cat Is Dead."

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.400
By the time "Police Academy" arrived in theaters,
Kim Cattrall had already co-starred in one

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.960
classic of the 1980s, playing a somewhat infamous
role in the 1981 comedy "Porky's."

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.210
As cadet Karen Thompson, she got to become
a little more of a fully-fledged character,

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.130
which led to more high-profile films throughout
the rest of the decade — though she didn't

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.630
return for any of the "Police Academy" sequels.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.630
"Why?"

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:35.770
"Because it's exciting - it's different!

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:41.390
It'll give me a chance to meet interesting
and unusual people."

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.700
Cattrall followed up "Police Academy" with
roles in "Big Trouble in Little China," "Mannequin,"

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:50.640
"Star Trek 6: The Undiscovered Country," and
more, but the role of her life was yet to

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.640
come.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.409
In 1998 she was cast as Samantha Jones in
the HBO comedy "Sex and the City," and her

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:00.530
performance as the sex-positive, outrageous
member of the show's key foursome made her

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:01.920
a fan favorite.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.610
She remained with the show throughout its
six-season run on HBO, and later returned

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.080
as Samantha for two feature films.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.010
Since "Sex and the City" ended, she has continued
working regularly, including roles in the

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.704
TV series "Producing Parker," "Sensitive Skin,"
"Modus," and "Tell Me a Story."

00:02:17.388 --> 00:02:21.790
George Gaynes' screen acting credits already
stretched back nearly 30 years by the time

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:27.180
he was cast as Commandant Lassard in "Police
Academy," and his work included roles in "Columbo,"

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.780
"Washington: Behind Closed Doors," and "Tootsie."

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:34.920
Playing Lassard brought Gaynes a new level
of visibility for 1980s moviegoers, and it

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.720
was the first step toward a new level of stardom.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.720
That new stardom also included a leading role
in the sitcom "Punky Brewster," in which he

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.870
played Henry Warnimont and voiced the character
again in an animated spinoff series.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:51.970
Between "Police Academy" and "Punky Brewster,"
Gaynes became an icon of 1980s pop culture.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:56.230
He continued working regularly throughout
the 1990s with roles in "The Days and Nights

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:01.330
of Molly Dodd," "Hearts of Fire," and continued
to play Lassard in all six "Police Academy"

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.210
sequels as well as a brief appearance in the
TV series.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:09.060
By the end of the 1990s, Gaynes had largely
retired from screen acting, and his last appearance

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.030
was a brief role in the 2003 comedy "Just
Married."

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.099
He died on February 15, 2016 at the age of
98.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.140
Michael Winslow started appearing in films
in 1980, and co-starred in two Cheech &amp; Chong

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.959
films before "Police Academy" rolled around
in 1984.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:30.870
As cadet Larvelle Jones, Winslow's incredible
talent for generating seemingly any sound

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:35.459
effect with only his mouth made him one of
the standout stars, and cemented his place

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:39.160
in the pop culture of the 1980s.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:46.210
"(noises) "Game over".

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.300
Winslow continued to play Jones in every subsequent
sequel.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.550
He also returned to play Larvelle Jones as
a police sergeant in the "Police Academy"

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.819
TV series in 1997.

00:03:55.819 --> 00:04:00.760
Outside the "Police Academy" franchise, Winslow
has kept busy ever since, with roles in "The

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.500
Love Boat," "Spaceballs," "Harry and the Hendersons,"
and more.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.410
Unsurprisingly, he's also a gifted voice actor
who has loaned his talents to the "New Kids

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:13.099
on the Block" animated series, "Grand Theft
Auto: San Andreas," "Robot Chicken" and more.

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:17.859
He's also a stand-up comedy performer and
released a concert film in 1999.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:22.699
In addition to acting, Winslow is also a musician
who has released his own music and collaborated

00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:25.499
with acts including Run the Jewels and Victor
Wooten.

00:04:26.056 --> 00:04:30.490
Before "Police Academy," Leslie Easterbrook
was best known for playing Rhonda Lee, the

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:34.800
neighbor of the title characters of the hit
sitcom "Laverne &amp; Shirley," for its final

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.020
three seasons between 1980 and 1983.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.020
As Sgt.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:43.199
Callahan in "Police Academy," she played the
tough-as-nails woman in a man's job who also

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.860
had a secret side, and while she didn't return
for "Police Academy 2," she did reprise her

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.210
role for the remaining five sequels and the
TV series.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.469
After "Police Academy," Easterbrook continued
working regularly, racking up dozens of television

00:04:55.469 --> 00:05:00.159
appearances in the 1980s and 1990s, including
guest spots on "Murder, She Wrote," "Hanging

00:05:00.159 --> 00:05:02.740
with Mr. Cooper," "Hunter," "Matlock," and
"Baywatch."

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:08.210
In 2005 she took over the role of Mother Firefly
in Rob Zombie's "The Devil's Rejects," and

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:12.139
since then has become something of a scream
queen, appearing in horror films including

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:16.319
Zombie's own "Halloween" remake, "The Afflicted,"and
"Sorority Party Massacre."

00:05:17.103 --> 00:05:22.419
G.W. Bailey was already an established character
actor with guest starring roles on "St. Elsewhere,"

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:27.169
"M*A*S*H," "Goodnight, Beantown," and others
when he was cast as the brutal Lt. Harris

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:28.169
in "Police Academy."

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.129
"You people are going to hate my guts for
the rest of your lives!

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:37.400
I am going to make you sorry that you ever
came here."

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.449
He continued to play Lt. Harris throughout
the "Police Academy" franchise, and has worked

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:42.910
regularly ever since.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:47.180
Among Bailey's numerous screen credits are
the films "Short Circuit," "Mannequin," and

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.770
a voice role in the animated "Home on the
Range."

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:54.319
He has appeared even more frequently on television,
including the miniseries "War and Remembrance"

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:58.180
and the series "The Jeff Foxworthy Show,"
"Murder, She Wrote," "American Dreams," and

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.689
"The Closer."

00:05:59.689 --> 00:06:05.580
In "The Closer," he played LAPD Detective
Lt. Louie Provenza for 109 episodes, then

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:10.210
crossed over into the TNT drama series "Major
Crimes," where he played the character until

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.960
the series ended in 2018.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:17.780
David Graf spent several years working primarily
as a guest actor on episodes of various TV

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:22.580
series before he was cast in the career-defining
role of Tackleberry in "Police Academy."

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.530
Tackleberry, a gung-ho security guard who
couldn't wait to join the police force and

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:32.029
seemed to always be armed and ready to shoot
something, was a key archetype in the original

00:06:32.029 --> 00:06:36.039
film, and became one of the franchise's most
recognizable characters.

00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:38.819
"Son, where did you get this gun?"

00:06:38.819 --> 00:06:41.580
"My mom gave it to me."

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:46.069
Graf returned to reprise the role for all
six sequels, and co-starred in the "Police

00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:48.789
Academy" television series in the late 1990s.

00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:53.740
Graf continued working regularly in film and
television throughout the 1980s and 1990s.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:59.620
In 1986 he co-starred in the short-lived sitcom
"He's The Mayor," and in the 1990s and early

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.509
2000s he appeared frequently in TV series
including "Seinfeld," "Home Improvement,"

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:05.979
"Step by Step," and "Star Trek: Deep Space
Nine."

00:07:05.979 --> 00:07:10.560
He was also an accomplished voice actor, appearing
in series and video games including "Aaahh!

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.619
Real Monsters!," and "The Wild Thornberrys."

00:07:12.619 --> 00:07:17.020
He died suddenly from a heart attack on April
7, 2001, at the age of 50.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:23.169
An imposing figure but a lovable guy, Moses
Hightower is probably the most memorable and

00:07:23.169 --> 00:07:26.939
beloved character from the entire "Police
Academy" franchise.

00:07:26.939 --> 00:07:31.960
It's the signature role of Charles "Bubba"
Smith, an NFL star in the 1960s and 1970s

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.119
who pivoted to playing tall tough guys on
sitcoms and in movies.

00:07:36.119 --> 00:07:40.129
Following "Police Academy," Smith reprised
his role in five sequel movies and the spinoff

00:07:40.129 --> 00:07:45.080
TV show, co-starred on short-lived shows including
"Blue Thunder" and "Half Nelson," and turned

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:49.389
in appearances in "Taxi," "Hart To Hart,"
"Married… with Children," and "Coach."

00:07:49.389 --> 00:07:54.440
In August 2011, 66-year-old Smith was found
dead at his Los Angeles home.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.529
According to the Los Angeles Times, the L.A.
County Coroner's office conducted an autopsy

00:07:58.529 --> 00:08:02.930
that concluded the actor died of an overdose
of weight-loss medication and also suffered

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:06.309
from blocked arteries, high blood pressure,
and an enlarged heart.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:11.849
"Police Academy" is notable for having not
only an ensemble of likable hero characters,

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:17.039
but a similar ensemble of unlikable villainous
characters, including Scott Thomson's cadet

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:18.039
Chad Copeland.

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:22.400
Thomson, who before "Police Academy" also
appeared in another '80s classic, "Fast Times

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.249
at Ridgemont High," hasn't appeared in as
many "Police Academy" installments as some

00:08:26.249 --> 00:08:30.429
of his co-stars, but he still reprised the
role in the third and fourth movies.

00:08:30.429 --> 00:08:33.740
Thomson has continued working in film and
television since the franchise.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.580
His TV roles include "Star Trek: The Next
Generation," "Hunter," "Parker Lewis Can't

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:41.490
Lose," and "True Blood," while his film roles
include "My Summer Story," "Night of the Living

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.910
Dead 3-D: Re-Animation," and "Greater."

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:49.260
He also matched "Police Academy" with an appearance
in one of the most popular films of the 1990s,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.680
playing the classical music-blaring storm
chaser Preacher in "Twister."

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.250
After a couple of sitcom and soap opera gigs
in the late 1970s, Marion Ramsey finally found

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:03.560
a breakout role as the squeaky-voiced and
deceptively powerful cadet Laverne Hooks.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.090
"Don't move dirtbag!"

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:11.070
She made her debut as Hooks in 1984's "Police
Academy" and went on to reprise the role in

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:14.160
five sequels, becoming a firm fan favorite.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.390
She didn't appear in the final "Police Academy"
movie, 1994's "Mission to Moscow," because

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.990
producers couldn't find a place for her character.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:25.020
This came as bad news for Ramsey, who was
going through financial troubles at the time.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.440
She would ultimately take out a loan from
"Police Academy" co-star Bubba Smith, who

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.460
dropped out of the film over Ramsey's omission.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.310
Ramsey would ride out the '90s and early 2000s
with small parts and voice acting work, popping

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:41.180
up on "Beverly Hills, 90210," "Robot Chicken,"
and the animated TV version of "The Addams

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:42.180
Family."

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.330
Her last role came in the musical docudrama
"When I Sing" in 2018, preceding her death

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:48.770
in 2021.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:49.770
She was 73.

00:09:50.291 --> 00:09:55.550
Captain Reed, the one-time boss of Cadet Mahoney's
father, sets the plot of "Police Academy"

00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:00.070
in motion, offering Mahoney a choice of punishment
for his crimes: He can go to prison, or he

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.580
can go legit by joining the Academy.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.700
Actor Ted Ross, best known for his Tony Award-winning
work as the Lion in the Broadway musical "The

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:13.210
Wiz" and its 1978 film adaptation, played
Captain Reed in the first "Police Academy,"

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.610
but he didn't return for any of the sequels.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:20.020
He wrapped up a long career in show business
not long after the film's 1984 release with

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.360
appearances in "Arthur 2: On the Rocks" "The
Cosby Show," and "The Fisher King."

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.270
He retired from acting in 1991 and moved back
to his hometown of Dayton, Ohio.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:34.220
In 1997, he opened a jazz club called Your
Place, where he'd perform on occasion.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:40.349
After suffering a stroke in 1998 that left
him homebound, Ross died at age 68 in 2002.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:45.460
Donovan Scott's screen acting career began
with a number of smaller roles in films before

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.710
he was cast as Leslie Barbara in "Police Academy."

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:52.690
Like Cattrall, Scott was one of the original
cast members who did not return for other

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.470
installments in the franchise.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:58.520
Since "Police Academy," Scott has worked regularly
in film and television.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:02.730
His film roles include "Savannah Smiles" "Splash,
Too," and "Blast from the Past," while his

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:07.380
TV roles include "Life with Lucy," "Alaska
Kid," "Babylon 5," "Boston Legal," "My Name

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.210
is Earl," and many others.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:13.660
In recent years, Scott has taken a liking
to playing Santa Claus, and frequently appears

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.720
as Santa in various films and television series.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.210
His many Santa appearances include episodes
of "Frasier," "It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia,"

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:26.251
"The Middle," "Life In Pieces," and "Superstore,"
as well as TV movies like "Northpole: Open

00:11:26.251 --> 00:11:28.130
for Christmas" and "Santa Switch."

00:11:28.814 --> 00:11:32.830
The character "Mr. Wig", played by Don Lake,
makes a brief but important appearance in

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:37.260
the first "Police Academy" movie: It's his
car that Carey Mahoney wrecks while working

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:39.260
as a parking lot attendant.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:43.740
After "Police Academy," Lake established himself
as a recognizable character actor, becoming

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.020
a familiar face with appearances in a number
of major comedies.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:51.700
Lake was a member of the troupe associated
with the mockumentaries of Christopher Guest,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:57.030
and co-starred in "A Mighty Wind," "Waiting
for Guffman," "Best In Show," "For Your Consideration,"

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:02.060
and "Mascot" while also popping up in "Terminator
2" and "Return to Me," which he co-wrote with

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:04.130
frequent collaborator Bonnie Hunt.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:08.450
Lake wrote for Hunt's talk show and sitcom
"Life With Bonnie," and they played the parents

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:11.280
of Officer Judy Hopps in Disney's "Zootopia."

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:15.910
More recently, Lake appeared on "Space Force"
as incompetent general Brad Gregory.

00:12:16.649 --> 00:12:21.720
Cadet Doug Fackler is responsible for some
of the wackiest antics in the first "Police

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:22.720
Academy" movie.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.780
He's played to perfection by Bruce Mahler.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:31.260
"Does the radio bother you?

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.790
I can turn it down."

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:37.360
After his screen comedy career was launched
in earnest as a member of the cast of ABC's

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.440
late night sketch show "Fridays," Mahler played
Sgt.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.050
Doug Fackler in four "Police Academy" movies.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:47.870
He went on to play some small parts on TV
and in movies before finding his second recognizable

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:52.660
role: Rabbi Glickman, a friend and counselor
to Elaine Benes on "Seinfeld," with his other

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:56.910
notable role coming in "Friday the 13th: The
Final Chapter" He hasn't been in a feature

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:01.940
film since 2000, preferring instead to spend
time with his family and raise border terrier

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:02.440
dogs.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:07.360
Portraying Cadet Kyle Blankes in "Police Academy"
was the first feature film work for actor

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:12.840
Brant von Hoffman, and he returned to the
franchise for 1986's "Police Academy 3: Back

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:13.840
in Training."

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.280
He'd dabble in film and television over the
next two decades, showing up in the films

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:21.990
"Rustlers' Rhapsody," "Blast from the Past,"
and "Dudley Do-Right," and on the TV series

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:24.660
"Home Improvement," "Arli$$," and "Arrested
Development."

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:29.080
He hasn't acted on screen since 2004, having
moved into a career as a writer.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:33.960
With his brother, Todd von Hoffmann, the former
actor wrote two humorous pop culture compendiums:

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.480
"Big Damn Book of Sheer Manliness" and "Bigger
Damner Book of Sheer Manliness."

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.810
Chief of police in the unidentified city of
"Police Academy" and then later police commissioner,

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:48.840
Henry Hurst is the institutional antagonist
of the franchise, at least initially.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:53.070
He's adamantly against his mayor's idea to
open up the Police Academy to a wider variety

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:54.070
of candidates.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:58.210
He hatches the plan to make them all quit,
but after it doesn't work, comes to appreciate

00:13:58.210 --> 00:13:59.380
the new cadets.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:03.850
The acting career of George R. Robertson is
notable for two things — portraying Henry

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:09.620
Hurst in six "Police Academy" movies between
1984 and 1989 and for playing politicians,

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.230
both fictional and real.

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:15.830
Robertson was President Davis in "Senior Trip,"
Senator Barry Goldwater in "The Reagans,"

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:19.140
and Vice President Dick Cheney in "The Path
to 9/11."

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:23.180
Robertson stopped taking on as many roles
when he reached his eighties, with one of

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:25.340
his last roles coming in the series "Haven."

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:30.970
Cadet George Martin's main arc as an officer
isn't one of finding a job or personal purpose,

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:35.290
but one of romance: He's ditching his relentless
pursuit of as many young women as possible

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:38.420
in order to get together with trainer Debbie
Callahan.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:42.191
Andrew Rubin was a seasoned character actor
by the time he played Cadet George Martin

00:14:42.191 --> 00:14:47.080
in the first "Police Academy," a career peak
that followed stints on '70s TV shows like

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:51.810
"Mary Hartman, Mary Hartman," "S.W.A.T.,"
"The Odd Couple," "Shazam!," and "The Jeffersons."

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:55.990
After "Police Academy," Rubin landed main
cast parts on the short-lived drama series

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:59.980
"Hometown" and "Joe Bash," then walked away
from acting for about a decade to head up

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.980
multiple non-profit and humanitarian organizations.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:08.170
In 2015, Rubin died of lung cancer at the
age of 69, according to The Hollywood Reporter.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:13.020
The main bad guy of the original "Police Academy"
movie is never given a name.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:18.940
Instead, he's a common, intimidating, leather-jacket-and-beanie-clad
street thug caricature officially listed in

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:20.950
the credits as "Main Bad Guy."

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:25.930
The part of Main Bad Guy was played by prolific
character actor and bit player Doug Lennox,

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:30.670
who returned to the series in "Police Academy
3: Back in Training" to portray "Axe Murderer."

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:34.710
He notched a few dozen credits in his time,
most notably popping up in "X-Men," "Lars

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:37.250
and the Real Girl," and "Against the Ropes."

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:41.700
He appeared in numerous television shows produced
in his native Canada, including "My Secret

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.800
Identity," "Night Heat," "Top Cops," and "Due
South."

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:49.790
Also a radio host and an author Lennox died
in 2015 at age 77.

00:15:50.411 --> 00:15:55.589
Violet Fackler knows that her husband Doug
has a tendency to slip up and create mayhem.

00:15:55.589 --> 00:15:59.440
In one memorable scene from the first "Police
Academy," she jumps on the hood of her husband's

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.030
car to prevent him from driving to the academy
sign-ups, only to get hurt in the process.

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:08.890
"Police Academy" is an iconic '80s movie,
and Debralee Scott was an iconic television

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:12.670
personality in her day: She made frequent
appearances on the celebrity-powered game

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:17.070
shows of the era, including "Family Feud,"
"Match Game," and "Password Plus."

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:21.200
As a young actor, she appeared on "The Love
Boat," had a recurring role on "Welcome Back,

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.371
Kotter," and she also played the title character's
sister, Cathy Shumway, on the soap spoof "Mary

00:16:26.371 --> 00:16:28.010
Hartman, Mary Hartman."

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:32.210
Scott's career came to an end not long after
she reprised the role of Violet Fackler in

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:34.880
1986's "Police Academy 3: Back in Training."

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.450
She focused on her personal life and became
engaged to New York City police officer Dennis

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:44.520
Levi, who died at the World Trade Center during
the 9/11 terrorist attacks, according to Contact

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:45.520
Music.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:49.380
Scott's family reported that the tragedy sent
the retired actor into a depression that led

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:52.780
her to drink heavily, which resulted in cirrhosis
of the liver.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.780
Scott died in April 2005 at age 52.

